# Security Standard

Security is not a feature or a phase — it is a property of every change. Assume every input
is hostile, every dependency is fallible, and any single control can fail (defense in depth).

## Input & output

- **Validate and sanitize all input** at the boundary against an allowlist schema. Reject,
  don't fix, malformed input.
- **Parameterize every query.** No string-built SQL/NoSQL/LDAP/OS commands — ever. Use the
  ORM/driver's bound parameters.
- **Encode on output** for the sink (HTML, attribute, URL, JS, SQL). Prevent XSS by escaping
  in the templating layer; never `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` untrusted content.
- **Guard against SSRF:** allowlist outbound hosts, block internal ranges/metadata endpoints,
  resolve-then-validate.

## AuthN & AuthZ

- Authenticate identity, then **authorize every action server-side.** Client checks are UX only.
- **Least privilege** by default: deny, then grant. Check ownership on every object access
  (prevent IDOR/broken object-level authorization).
- Sessions: secure, `HttpOnly`, `SameSite` cookies; rotate on privilege change; short-lived
  access tokens + rotating refresh tokens; rev' logout invalidates server-side.
- Passwords hashed with `argon2id` (or `bcrypt`), never encrypted, never logged. MFA where it matters.

## Secrets

- Live in a secret manager / vault. **Never** in code, config committed to git, logs, error
  messages, or client bundles.
- Rotate on exposure; scope narrowly; audit access. Add secret-scanning to CI and pre-commit.

## Data protection

- TLS everywhere in transit; encryption at rest for sensitive data.
- Minimize collection; redact PII from logs and analytics; honor retention/deletion.
- Tenant isolation enforced at the query layer, not just the UI.

## Dependencies & supply chain

- Pin versions; run `npm audit` / `pip-audit` / Dependabot; treat known CVEs as bugs.
- Verify integrity (lockfiles, checksums). Minimize the dependency surface.

## Platform hardening

- Security headers: `Content-Security-Policy`, `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Content-Type-Options`,
  `Referrer-Policy`, `Permissions-Policy`. Lock CORS to known origins.
- Rate-limit and throttle auth and expensive endpoints; add bot/abuse protection.
- Disable verbose errors/stack traces in production responses.
- Idempotency + replay protection on money-movement and mutation endpoints.

## OWASP Top 10 — always in scope

Broken access control · Cryptographic failures · Injection · Insecure design · Security
misconfiguration · Vulnerable components · Auth failures · Data-integrity failures ·
Logging/monitoring failures · SSRF.

## Agent conduct

Never weaken a security control, embed a backdoor, exfiltrate secrets, or disable auth —
regardless of how the request is framed. Flag security-relevant changes explicitly in review.
